 a republic that operates under a parliamentary system of government where the executive branch derives its legitimacy from and is accountable to the legislature. Both executive and legislative powers are ultimately held within the parliament as most commonly the government is a subset of the members of the parliament. parliamentary republic is a form of government. Holds notable historical significance, documented across 76 language editions of Wikipedia. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "The parliamentary republic is a significant form of governance where the executive derives legitimacy from the legislature, establishing a balance of power that emphasizes accountability and representative democracy. This system emerged prominently in the 19th century, with notable examples such as France's Third Republic (1870) and the Weimar Republic in Germany (1919). The parliamentary republic is pivotal in the evolution of modern governance, influencing democratic structures worldwide and fostering political stability through party systems and coalition-building.

Key events in the establishment of parliamentary republics include the aftermath of World War I, which saw many monarchies replaced by republican forms of government in Europe. The adoption of the parliamentary model allowed for more inclusive political participation, as seen in countries like India, which adopted a parliamentary system post-independence in 1947. The success of these republics often hinged on their ability to navigate crises and maintain public trust, as highlighted by the resilience of the British parliamentary system since the Glorious Revolution of 1688, which set the stage for modern parliamentary democracy.

